{"w":2400,"h":3395,"lines":[{"w":0.053497943878173831,"h":0.013250883392226132,"c":1,"t":"Test 1","y":0.10895170803418419,"x":0.20987654236132461},{"h":0.019249126402874017,"c":1,"x":0.2076855634620273,"y":0.14874465425144012,"t":"something to complain about in relation to their workplace (Poulston, 2009). There is a strong","w":0.70193155924479167},{"y":0.16401972164379353,"h":0.018187944122422306,"w":0.71424845377604174,"c":1,"t":"link between the perceptions of employees and particular factors of their work environment that","x":0.20972275191551396},{"t":"are separate from the work itself, including company policies, salary and vacations.","x":0.20973691096163122,"w":0.62374308268229162,"c":1,"h":0.018242742765356978,"y":0.17956725258827377},{"h":0.019530382819113901,"c":1,"y":0.21026065776686675,"t":"Such conditions are particularly troubling for the luxury hotel market, where high-quality service,","x":0.20767479294877469,"w":0.73280227661132813},{"h":0.016976824918821132,"c":1,"w":0.68538319905598966,"y":0.22606210253212455,"t":"requiring a sophisticated approach to HRM, is recognized as a critical source of competitive","x":0.20977934814023635},{"w":0.68749430338541673,"t":"advantage (Maroudas et al., 2008). In a real sense, the services of hotel employees represent","x":0.20769049849086721,"y":0.24118033161057395,"h":0.019646508674037499,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.20975523722096889,"t":"their industry (Schneider and Bowen, 1993). This representation has commonly been limited to","h":0.016770868896735847,"y":0.25707178716626444,"w":0.70803161621093746},{"y":0.27184763076935459,"h":0.018010257972564503,"c":1,"x":0.20978387687884975,"t":"guest experiences. This suggests that there has been a dichotomy between the guest environment","w":0.71828653971354184},{"x":0.20763433909706522,"h":0.019660408280623098,"w":0.54358261108398442,"t":"provided in luxury hotels and the working conditions of their employees.","y":0.28729096453235758,"c":1},{"x":0.20559250739484611,"w":0.68755167643229176,"c":1,"y":0.31777948438667147,"t":"It is therefore essential for hotel management to develop HRM practices that enable them to","h":0.02066341530448268},{"w":0.67308603922526034,"c":1,"y":0.33361384866055666,"x":0.20564450359165695,"h":0.019139940241621578,"t":"inspire and retain competent employees. This requires an understanding of what motivates"},{"h":0.018079084838939163,"w":0.72868789672851564,"y":0.34846771333625459,"t":"employees at different levels of management and different stages of their careers (Enz and Siguaw,","c":1,"x":0.20974882445078094},{"y":0.36322383423447435,"w":0.7205367533365884,"h":0.021619539519221243,"t":"2000). This implies that it is beneficial for hotel managers to understand what practices are most","c":1,"x":0.20555573575446004},{"c":1,"x":0.20767931308083604,"t":"favorable to increase employee satisfaction and retention.","y":0.38024212631638843,"w":0.42825281778971352,"h":0.015082788411242554},{"t":"Herzberg (1966) proposes that people have two major types of needs, the first being extrinsic","y":0.40975001695678426,"h":0.020234362958317265,"w":0.6937325032552083,"x":0.20559590004944159,"c":1},{"x":0.20774838827996092,"h":0.015687519024061514,"y":0.42699629760202207,"c":1,"w":0.67092971801757828,"t":"motivation factors relating to the context in which work is performed, rather than the work"},{"y":0.43962578784029893,"w":0.69796920776367177,"h":0.021958093901545461,"c":1,"t":"itself. These include working conditions and job security. When these factors are unfavorable,","x":0.20554175269899824},{"x":0.20356434753740946,"y":0.4571549403059596,"t":"job dissatisfaction may result. Significantly, though, just fulfilling these needs does not result in","h":0.019195233132729639,"c":1,"w":0.7121829732259114},{"x":0.20354019663158376,"t":"satisfaction, but only in the reduction of dissatisfaction (Maroudas et al., 2008).","c":1,"w":0.59706253051757818,"y":0.47128557063298548,"h":0.020422020003427743},{"x":0.207665116821107,"y":0.50208444823139531,"w":0.68340627034505208,"h":0.018346268661732612,"t":"Employees also have intrinsic motivation needs or motivators, which include such factors as","c":1},{"t":"achievement and recognition. Unlike extrinsic factors, motivator factors may ideally result in job","x":0.20566333357581887,"h":0.017657889353793643,"w":0.71829065958658855,"c":1,"y":0.51879163399190842},{"t":"satistaction (Maroudas et al., 2008). Herzberg's (1966) theory discusses the need for a 'balance'","w":0.71810699462890626,"x":0.20370370423118458,"h":0.017667844522968212,"y":0.53474676067055227,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"of these two types of needs.","w":0.20816661834716793,"y":0.54926512811772554,"h":0.014158137695528206,"x":0.20560527109157312},{"x":0.20560739925443253,"w":0.68346430460611984,"h":0.018254510365049215,"c":1,"y":0.57918431501334833,"t":"The impact of fun as a motivating factor at work has also been explored. For example, Tews,"},{"y":0.59541674260621003,"t":"Michel and Stafford (2013) conducted a study focusing on staff from a chain of themed restaurants","c":1,"h":0.016745449376190491,"w":0.72859573364257812,"x":0.20564981962446191},{"c":1,"w":0.70606674194335939,"y":0.60967118135895471,"x":0.20354696534499625,"h":0.018130607964433665,"t":"in the United States. It was found that fun activities had a favorable impact on performance and"},{"c":1,"y":0.62538894393336786,"x":0.20562014142956675,"h":0.018980976549559769,"w":0.70605194091796863,"t":"manager support for fun had a favorable impact in reducing turnover. Their findings support the"},{"t":"view that fun may indeed have a beneficial effect, but the framing of that fun must be carefully","c":1,"x":0.20149637098973963,"w":0.69990875244140627,"h":0.020509701875971897,"y":0.64084071657946984},{"t":"aligned with both organizational goals and employee characteristics. 'Managers must learn how to","x":0.20357142935238604,"y":0.65661038763284318,"c":1,"w":0.7286417134602865,"h":0.01953285630656354},{"x":0.20363908643650877,"y":0.67286662843756539,"w":0.72852564493815108,"h":0.01595747400650005,"c":1,"t":"achieve the delicate balance of allowing employees the freedom to enjoy themselves at work while"},{"w":0.56196431477864595,"t":"simultaneously maintaining high levels of performance' (Tews et al., 2013).","x":0.20360088176530117,"y":0.68696629134187592,"c":1,"h":0.018272119079517979},{"t":"Deery (2008) has recommended several actions that can be adopted at the organizational level to","x":0.20559154519606807,"y":0.71768913036917059,"c":1,"w":0.71633748372395833,"h":0.018836492644041525},{"x":0.20361029600864564,"h":0.016400855618174615,"c":1,"t":"retain good staff as well as assist in balancing work and family life. Those particularly appropriate","y":0.73437061005854476,"w":0.72651438395182288},{"x":0.20357759647616111,"h":0.017224919529208349,"y":0.74924774065866517,"w":0.66073287963867189,"c":1,"t":"to the hospitality industry include allowing adequate breaks during the working day, staff"},{"t":"functions that involve families, and providing health and well-being opportunities.","h":0.019557182303025389,"c":1,"w":0.61345011393229165,"y":0.76447296333898906,"x":0.20151003726933009},{"w":0.024691357612609849,"t":"26","h":0.011627907185728592,"y":0.86482558123800246,"c":1,"x":0.19958847747876804}]}